namespace Ponca
{
  /*!
    \page pyponca %PyPonca: A python binding of Ponca

    <center>[\ref user_manual_page "Go back to user manual"]</center>

    
    \section pyponca_intro PyPonca

    Starting with version 2.0, Ponca now has a Python binding that is available within this repository. 
    The binding is compatible with most numerical libraries (NumPy, PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX, etc.) and retains the performance of the C++ code. 
    It provides an interface (quite different from the C++ API, see \ref pyponca_dev for explanation) to the computation that Ponca supports. 

    \subsection pyponca_install How to install

    Currently, there are no pypi release and the library must be installed manually. Only a C++ compiler, cmake
    and python development librairies are required. Once those installed, run the following commands to install
    the library within your current python environment:

    \code{.sh}
        cd Pyponca/
        pip install .
    \endcode

    You may test afterward the installation using the command: 

    \code{.sh}
        python -c "import pyponca"
    \endcode

    \subsection pyponca_api API and first examples

    The python module exposes a small set of objects:
     - `pyponca.PointCloud`: a lightweight, non-owning view over a position array (and optionally a normal array),
     - `pyponca.KDTree`: a spatial partitioning structure built over a point cloud, used to speed up neighbor queries,
     - one class per fitting method available in Ponca (e.g. `pyponca.APSS`, `pyponca.CovariancePlaneFit`, ...),
       called *compute objects* in the following. The full list is available in `pyponca.ComputeObjectList`,
     - `pyponca.Filters`: the neighbor filter (weighting scheme) to use,
     - `pyponca.Computation`: the list of quantities that can be extracted from a fit,
     - `pyponca.FitResult`: the status of a fit (`STABLE`, `UNSTABLE` or `UNDEFINED`).

    Unlike the C++ API, a compute object is not fitted once and then queried. Instead, the user describes, ahead of
    time, <b>all</b> the neighborhoods to process and <b>all</b> the quantities to extract. A single call to
    `compute` then performs every fit and returns the requested results as arrays, along with the status of each fit.
    This keeps loops on the C++ side (see \ref pyponca_why for the rationale). The typical workflow is:

    \code{.py}
        fit = pyponca.<Method>()                         # 1. Choose a fitting method
        fit.setNeighborFilter(centers, radii)            # 2. Describe the neighborhoods (one fit per center)
        fit.addComputation(pyponca.Computation.<ID>)     # 3. Describe what to extract (as many times as needed)
        fit.addComputation(pyponca.Computation.<ID>, input)
        output = fit.compute(points)                     # 4. Run every fit
        output.results                                   #    One array per computation, in order
        output.fitStatus                                 #    The pyponca.FitResult of each fit
    \endcode

    \subsubsection pyponca_api_normals Example: normal estimation with a plane fit

    This example estimates a normal and a local mean position at every point of a point cloud, using a covariance plane
    fit over a ball of radius \f$0.1\f$ centered on each point.

    \snippet pyponca_normal_estimation.py normal_estimation

    \subsubsection pyponca_api_apss Example: APSS projection and curvature

    Oriented methods, such as APSS, require normals. Here, a sphere is fitted around a set of query locations, the
    locations are projected onto the fitted surface, and the GLS parameters (which contain a curvature estimate) are
    extracted.

    \snippet pyponca_apss.py apss

    \subsubsection pyponca_api_kdtree Example: neighbor queries

    The `KDTree` can also be used on its own to perform range queries:

    \snippet pyponca_kdtree_queries.py kdtree_queries

    \subsubsection pyponca_api_libs Using other numerical libraries

    %PyPonca relies on <a href="https://nanobind.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank">nanobind</a> and the
    <a href="https://dmlc.github.io/dlpack/latest/" target="_blank">DLPack</a> protocol: inputs can be any array
    exposing DLPack (NumPy, PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX, CuPy, ...), and outputs are DLPack arrays that can be converted
    without copy using the `from_dlpack` function of the target library.

    \snippet pyponca_torch.py torch

    \subsection pyponca_manual User manual

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_types Supported data

    The binding is instantiated for:
     - scalar types: `float32` and `float64`,
     - dimensions: 2 and 3 (some methods are only available in 3D, see \ref pyponca_manual_methods).

    All arrays given to the same computation (point cloud, filter centers, radii and computation inputs) are expected
    to share the same scalar type and to live on the same device. The type of the point cloud determines which C++
    specialization is used. Arrays do not need to be contiguous: arbitrary strides (including negative ones) are
    supported on every dimension, and no copy is performed.

    \note Only CPU arrays are currently supported. Computations on other devices raise a `RuntimeError`.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_pointcloud Point clouds

    \snippet pyponca_user_manual.py pointcloud

    `pos` and `normals` must be 2D arrays of shape `(N, D)` with the same shape and dtype; no implicit conversion is
    performed, mismatching inputs raise a `RuntimeError` or `TypeError`. A `PointCloud` does not own its data: the
    arrays must be kept alive as long as the point cloud (or a `KDTree` built on it) is in use.

    When no normals are given, they are set to the null vector. Methods that rely on normals (such as `APSS`,
    `MeanNormal`, ...) will then return meaningless values.

    Every function that accepts a `PointCloud` also accepts a raw `(N, D)` array, which is converted to a
    `PointCloud` without normals.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_kdtree KDTree

    \snippet pyponca_user_manual.py kdtree

    The second argument is forwarded to the C++ binding as its `dense` parameter: `True` (the default) builds a
    Ponca::KdTreeDense, `False` builds a Ponca::KdTreeSparse. Both return the same results; see
    \ref spatialpartitioning_kdtree for the difference between the two.

    `rangeNeighbors` has two overloads:
     - `rangeNeighbors(pos, radius)`, with `pos` of shape `(D,)` and `radius` a scalar: returns a 1D `int64` array
       containing the indices of the points within the ball,
     - `rangeNeighbors(pos, radii)`, with `pos` of shape `(M, D)` and `radii` of shape `(M,)` (or `(1,)`, broadcast to
       every query): returns an `int64` array of shape `(M, K)`, where `K` is the largest neighborhood size. Each row
       is padded with `-1`.

    When given to `compute`, a `KDTree` restricts each fit to the points returned by `rangeNeighbors(center, radius)`.
    With a plain `PointCloud`, every point of the cloud is visited for every fit and the neighbor filter alone decides
    which points contribute. The `KDTree` is therefore much faster for large point clouds.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_methods Compute objects

    Each fitting method is exposed as a class in the `pyponca` namespace, named after the corresponding entry of the
    Ponca::Factory. The following methods are available (`pyponca.ComputeObjectList` contains the list of names
    actually compiled in the module):

    | Name                                  | Dimension  | Description                                                     |
    |---------------------------------------|------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
    | `DryFit`                              | 2D, 3D     | Does nothing, mostly useful for testing                         |
    | `MeanPosition`                        | 2D, 3D     | Weighted mean position of the neighborhood                      |
    | `MeanNormal`                          | 2D, 3D     | Weighted mean normal of the neighborhood                        |
    | `CovarianceLineFit`                   | 2D, 3D     | Line fitted using the covariance of positions                   |
    | `CovariancePlaneFit`                  | 2D, 3D     | Plane fitted using the covariance of positions                  |
    | `MeanPlaneFit`                        | 2D, 3D     | Plane defined by the mean position and mean normal              |
    | `SphereFit`                           | 2D, 3D     | Algebraic sphere fitted to positions                            |
    | `APSS`                                | 2D, 3D     | Algebraic sphere fitted to positions and normals                |
    | `UnorientedAPSS`                      | 2D, 3D     | Algebraic sphere fitted to positions and unoriented normals     |
    | `MongePatchQuadratic`                 | 3D         | Quadratic height field in the tangent plane                     |
    | `MongePatchRestrictedQuadratic`       | 3D         | Restricted quadratic height field in the tangent plane          |
    | `CNCUniform`, `CNCIndependent`, `CNCHexagram`, `CNCAvgHexagram` | 3D | Corrected Normal Current curvature estimators  |
    | `ASO`                                 | 3D         | APSS with spatial derivatives, provides principal curvatures    |

    See \ref fittingreference for a description of each method. Compute objects take no argument at construction.
    The C++ specialization is only selected when `compute` is called, depending on the point cloud type and on the
    neighbor filter. Requesting a method that is not available for the data (e.g. a 3D-only method on a 2D point
    cloud) raises a `NotImplementedError`.

    A compute object provides three methods:
     - `setNeighborFilter(centers, radii, nf = pyponca.Filters.SMOOTH_WEIGHT, normals = None)`: sets the
       neighborhoods, see \ref pyponca_manual_filters,
     - `addComputation(id, input = None)`: appends a computation to perform after each fit, see
       \ref pyponca_manual_computations,
     - `compute(data)`: performs all fits on `data` (a `PointCloud`, a `KDTree` or a raw `(N, D)` array) and returns
       the results along with the status of each fit, see \ref pyponca_manual_results.

    Settings are kept between calls to `compute`: the same object can be run on several point clouds, or with a new
    neighbor filter. Calls to `addComputation` accumulate.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_filters Neighbor filters

    `setNeighborFilter(centers, radii, nf, normals)` describes the set of neighborhoods to process: one independent
    fit is performed for each center.
     - `centers` is an array of shape `(M, D)`. `M` does not need to match the size of the point cloud.
     - `radii` is a 1D array of shape `(M,)`, or of shape `(1,)` to use the same radius for every center. A plain
       python scalar is not accepted: use `np.array([r])`.
     - `nf` is the weighting scheme, among the `pyponca.Filters` listed below.
     - `normals` is an array of shape `(M, D)` holding one normal per center. It is only used by the CNC methods,
       see below.

    | Filter                            | C++ equivalent                                                 |
    |-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
    | `Filters.SMOOTH_WEIGHT` (default) | Ponca::DistWeightFilter with a Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel       |
    | `Filters.CONSTANT_WEIGHT`         | Ponca::DistWeightFilter with a Ponca::ConstantWeightKernel     |
    | `Filters.NO_WEIGHT`               | Ponca::NoWeightFilter                                          |

    `SMOOTH_WEIGHT` and `CONSTANT_WEIGHT` ignore points outside of the ball and express positions relative to the
    center. `NO_WEIGHT` gives a weight of \f$1\f$ to every point it is given and keeps global coordinates: with a raw
    `PointCloud`, all the points of the cloud are then used, while with a `KDTree` only the points within the radius
    are.

    CNC methods ignore `nf` and always use the equivalent of `CONSTANT_WEIGHT`. They also require `normals`, the
    expected surface normal at each center, which their curvature estimation relies on. Every other method ignores
    `normals`.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_computations Computations

    `addComputation(id, input)` requests the extraction of a quantity, identified by a value of `pyponca.Computation`,
    after each fit. In the following, `M` is the number of filter centers and `D` the dimension of the point cloud.

    | Computation                 | Input      | Output shape     | Content                                                                  |
    |-----------------------------|------------|------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | `NONE`                      | None       | `(M, 0)`         | Nothing, only performs the fits                                          |
    | `MEAN_POSITION`             | None       | `(M, D)`         | Weighted barycenter of the neighborhood                                  |
    | `MEAN_NORMAL`               | None       | `(M, D)`         | Weighted mean normal of the neighborhood                                 |
    | `POTENTIAL`                 | Optional   | `(M,)`           | Value of the fitted scalar field                                         |
    | `PRIMITIVE_GRADIENT`        | Optional   | `(M, D)`         | Gradient of the fitted scalar field (i.e. non normalized normal)         |
    | `PROJECTION`                | Required   | `(M, D)`         | Projection of the input point onto the fitted primitive                  |
    | `ALGEBRAIC_SPHERE`          | None       | `(M, 2 + D)`     | Radius, Pratt norm, then center of the fitted sphere                     |
    | `GLS`                       | None       | `(M, 2 + D)`     | GLS parameters \f$\tau\f$, \f$\kappa\f$, then \f$\eta\f$                  |
    | `GLS_NORMALIZED`            | None       | `(M, 2 + D)`     | Scale-normalized GLS parameters \f$\tau\f$, \f$\kappa\f$, then \f$\eta\f$ |
    | `PRINCIPAL_CURVATURE`       | None       | `(M, 3 + 2D)`    | \f$k_{min}\f$, \f$k_{max}\f$, Gaussian curvature, then the directions of \f$k_{min}\f$ and \f$k_{max}\f$ |
    | `QUADRATIC_HEIGHTFIELD_MAP` | Required   | `(M,)`           | Height of the fitted height field at the input \f$(u, v)\f$ coordinates  |

    Not every computation is available for every method: for instance, `ALGEBRAIC_SPHERE` requires a sphere fit, and
    `PRINCIPAL_CURVATURE` requires a curvature estimator (`ASO`, CNC methods, ...). Requesting an invalid combination
    raises a `RuntimeError` (or a `ValueError` when the input is invalid) when `compute` is called. Refer to
    \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities to know which capabilities each method provides.

    For computations taking an input, the input must have the same dtype as the point cloud, and its last dimension
    must be `D` (or `2` for `QUADRATIC_HEIGHTFIELD_MAP`). Several layouts are accepted:
     - a single vector of shape `(D,)`: the same input is used for every fit, the output shape is as above,
     - an array of shape `(M, D)`: the `i`-th row is used for the `i`-th fit, the output shape is as above,
     - an array of shape `(M, K, D)`: `K` inputs are evaluated for each fit, and an extra dimension `K` is added to the
       output, e.g. `(M, K, D)` for `PROJECTION` or `(M, K)` for `POTENTIAL`. When `K` is 1, this extra dimension is
       dropped and the output shape is the same as for an `(M, D)` input.

    When no input is given to `POTENTIAL` or `PRIMITIVE_GRADIENT`, the quantity is evaluated at the filter center.

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_results Results

    `compute` returns a `ComputationResults` object with two attributes:
     - `results`: a list containing one array per call to `addComputation`, in the same order,
     - `fitStatus`: an `int32` array of shape `(M,)` holding the status of each fit, as a `pyponca.FitResult` value.

    All arrays are DLPack-compatible and allocated on the same device as the point cloud:

    \snippet pyponca_user_manual.py results

    `pyponca.FitResult` is an integer enumeration mirroring Ponca::FIT_RESULT, so its values compare directly with
    `fitStatus` (and `pyponca.FitResult(int(s))` gives the name of a status `s`):

    | Status                | Meaning                                                               |
    |-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | `FitResult.STABLE`    | The fit is stable and ready to use                                    |
    | `FitResult.UNSTABLE`  | The fit is ready to use, but considered unstable (e.g. few neighbors) |
    | `FitResult.UNDEFINED` | The fit failed (e.g. no neighbor): its results are not meaningful     |

    Results are written for every fit, whatever its status. Values computed from an `UNDEFINED` fit are not
    meaningful (they can be `NaN`, or arbitrary), so filter them out using `fitStatus`:

    \snippet pyponca_user_manual.py fit_status

    \subsubsection pyponca_manual_errors Errors

    The main errors raised by the binding are listed below. The arguments of a compute object are only checked when
    `compute` is called.
     - `NotImplementedError`: no specialization exists for the given data (unsupported dtype or dimension, or method
       not available in this dimension),
     - `TypeError`: an array does not match the expected dtype or shape of the selected specialization,
     - `RuntimeError`: invalid computation for the method, missing call to `addComputation`, or device mismatch
       between the point cloud and the other arrays,
     - `ValueError`: invalid neighborhoods (mismatching shapes or devices between `centers`, `radii` and `normals`,
       or missing `normals` for a CNC method), or invalid input for a computation (missing input for `PROJECTION`,
       unexpected input for `MEAN_POSITION`, ...).

    \section pyponca_dev Development documentation

    This section is meant for developers of the binding. Its sources are located in `PyPonca/`:
     - `binding/`: the C++ binding code, written with
       <a href="https://nanobind.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank">nanobind</a>. `Common/` holds the point cloud and
       the mangling functions, `Fitting/` the compute objects and computations, and `SpatialPartitioning/` the
       KD-tree,
     - `pyponca/`: the Python package, which dispatches calls to the C++ module `pyponca._pyponca`,
     - `tests/`: the test suites, see \ref pyponca_tests.

    `PyPonca/CMakeLists.txt` builds the `_pyponca` extension module, and is enabled by the `PONCA_CONFIGURE_PYPONCA`
    option of the main CMake project. `PyPonca/pyproject.toml` builds this project with scikit-build-core when
    running `pip install`.

    \subsection pyponca_why Why a different API?

    In C++, a compute object is fitted once, then queried for any quantity its capabilities provide:

    \code{.cpp}
        Fit fit;
        fit.setNeighborFilter({location, radius});
        fit.compute(points);

        // Query the fit, knowing its capabilities
        VectorType projection = fit.project(query);
    \endcode

    In Python, every neighborhood and every quantity to extract is described before the computation:

    \code{.py}
        fit = pyponca.Fit()
        fit.setNeighborFilter(locations, radii)  # All the neighborhoods at once
        fit.addComputation(id, input)            # What to extract from each fit
        fit.addComputation(id, input)
        output = fit.compute(points)             # Performs every fit
    \endcode

    This API keeps the loops on the C++ side. Iterating over the point cloud in Python, one fit at a time, would be
    slow and cumbersome to parallelize. Users therefore give all the filter centers and radii at once.

    Reproducing the C++ pattern on top of this is possible (and was tried), but leaves three options, each with a major
    drawback:
     - keep a copy of every fitted object, and extract the results from them afterwards. Memory usage is high, and
       most of the benefits of parallelization are lost: each extraction pays again the overhead of creating threads
       (or launching CUDA kernels),
     - discard the fitted objects, in which case every extraction requires fitting them again,
     - let users provide a function (e.g. a lambda) applied to each fitted object. Calling a Python function from C++
       for every fit is slow, as each call goes back and forth between Python and C++, and is not possible on CUDA
       devices. Avoiding this would require compiling user functions just in time along with Ponca's custom classes, 
       which extends beyond the scope of the library.

    Describing the computations before calling `compute` reconciles time and memory performance: each fit is
    performed once, its results are extracted right away, then the fitted object is discarded. The price is a loss of
    control: users are restricted to the operations listed in `pyponca.Computation` (see \ref pyponca_new_compute to
    add one). For the same reason, `compute` returns the status of each fit along with the results: once the fitted
    objects are discarded, it is the only way to know which results are valid.

    \subsection pyponca_mangling Name mangling

    Ponca relies on templates to provide generic yet efficient code. Templates are resolved at compile time, which is
    not directly compatible with Python: the binding must instantiate and expose every specialization that should be
    available. This is especially challenging in Ponca because the point type is itself a template parameter: every
    class templated on a point would need a different name for each scalar type and dimension, and users would have
    to pick a different class depending on their data.

    Instead, the binding relies on name mangling and on a dispatcher written in Python:
     - in C++, every specialization is registered in `pyponca._pyponca` under a unique mangled name, built from the
       name of the algorithm (e.g. `APSS`) and from its template parameters (e.g. a 3D `float` point gives `3f`),
     - in `pyponca/__init__.py`, a Python class is created for every algorithm listed in `pyponca.ComputeObjectList`.
       It records the calls to `setNeighborFilter` and `addComputation`. Once the type of the data is known, that is
       when `compute` is called, it computes the mangled name of the matching C++ class, instantiates it, replays the
       recorded calls and runs the computation. `pyponca.PointCloud` and `pyponca.KDTree` are dispatched the same way,
       from the arrays they are built on.

    The mangling rules below are implemented on both sides. To keep them consistent, use the functions of
    `binding/Common/Mangling.h` (e.g. `MangleArray`) on the C++ side, and `pyponca._pyponca.internal._mangleArray` on
    the Python side.

    \subsubsection pyponca_mangling_arrays Arrays and points

    The first dimension of an array is never encoded. The following dimensions are encoded as the list of their sizes,
    separated by underscores: an array of shape `(12, 8, 4)` gives `8_4`. The scalar type is then appended, using the
    first letter of the C++ type: `f` for `float`, `d` for `double`, and `unknown` for any other type. A `float` point
    cloud of shape `(N, 3)` is therefore encoded as `3f`.

    On the C++ side, the same name is computed from the point type by `ManglePoint` (dimension, then scalar type). It
    is followed by a suffix describing the kind of point. Only one kind is bound for now, points with positions and
    normals (`PointNormalBinding`), whose suffix is `PN`. The point cloud and KD-tree classes are named after it, e.g.
    `PointCloud3fPN` and `KdTree3fPN`.

    \subsubsection pyponca_mangling_filters Filters

    Filter names are chosen by hand in `binding/Fitting/Filters.h`, using short names so that class names remain
    readable: `SW` (smooth weight), `CW` (constant weight) and `NW` (no weight). They are the values of
    `pyponca.Filters`.

    \subsubsection pyponca_mangling_co Compute objects

    The name of a compute object is the concatenation of the name of the method, of the array mangling, of the point
    type mangling, then of the filter mangling. For instance, `MongePatchRestrictedQuadratic3fPNCW` is the
    `MongePatchRestrictedQuadratic` method for 3D `float` point clouds with normals, using the constant weight filter.

    Compute objects whose type does not depend on the neighbor filter, such as the CNC methods, are registered once,
    without filter suffix (e.g. `CNCUniform3fPN`). The dispatcher falls back to this name when no class with the
    filter suffix exists.

    \subsection pyponca_new_compute Adding compute objects and computations

    \subsubsection pyponca_new_compute_object Compute objects

    Compute objects are not listed by hand: `RegisterComputeObjects` (`binding/Fitting/RegisterFitting.cpp`)
    registers every entry of the Ponca::Factory for each scalar type (`float`, `double`), each dimension (2 and 3) and
    each filter of `binding/Fitting/Filters.h`. 2D point clouds get the generic list of the factory, 3D point clouds
    the 3D list with space derivatives (see `Ponca/src/Fitting/Factory/factorylist.h`). Adding an entry to these lists
    is therefore enough to make a method available in Python. The binding expects a compute object to:
     - be default constructible, and provide `init()`, `compute(begin, end)` and `computeWithIds(ids, points)`, the
       last two returning a Ponca::FIT_RESULT,
     - provide `setNeighborFilter(nf)` and `getNeighborFilter()`: when computing on a KD-tree, the binding collects
       the neighbors using the center (`frame().center()`) and the radius (`evalScale()`) of the filter,
     - define a `NeighborFilter` type, constructible either from a center and a radius, or from a center, a radius
       and a normal. In the latter case, `setNeighborFilter` requires `normals`.

    If the type of the new object does not depend on the neighbor filter, add its name to `nonTemplatedFilterTypes`
    in `RegisterComputeObjects`, so that it is registered once, without filter suffix. The computations available for
    an object are deduced from its capabilities (Ponca::ProvidesImplicitPrimitive, Ponca::ProvidesPrincipalCurvatures,
    ...), so nothing else needs to change.

    \subsubsection pyponca_new_computation Computations

    Computations are defined in `binding/Fitting/FittingList.h`. Adding one takes four steps:
     1. Add a value to the `Computation` enumeration, and register it under its Python name in `RegisterComputations`.
     2. In `ComputeOutputDimension`, add a case returning the shape of the output array, which is allocated before the
        fits run. Guard it with the capability it requires (`if constexpr (Ponca::ProvidesX<CO>)`): when the guard
        fails, the function raises an error for this invalid combination. Check the input there too, e.g. throw a
        `std::invalid_argument` (a `ValueError` in Python) when a required input is missing. Build the shape with
        `ComputeVectorDim<Point, T>(input)` for an output of type `T` (a scalar or a fixed-size Eigen matrix), which
        handles every input layout described in \ref pyponca_manual_computations, or with
        `ComputeVectorDimGroup<Point, T1, T2, ...>(input)` to concatenate several outputs on the last dimension, as
        `PRINCIPAL_CURVATURE` does. The first dimension is a placeholder, replaced by the number of filter centers.
     3. In `ExtractComputation`, add a case with the same guard, writing the results of the `i`-th fit. Without input,
        write at `out.data + i * out.stride[0]` (shifted by `k * out.stride[1]` for the `k`-th value of a group) with
        `Write`, which accepts scalars and Eigen matrices. With an input, call
        `RunMethod<Size, CO>(object, f, in, out, i)`, which handles the input layouts. `Size` is the size of one input,
        and `f` is created with `MAKE_VECTOR_COMPUTE_FUNCTION(method)` for a vector input, or with
        `MAKE_COMPUTE_FUNCTION_1(method)` and `MAKE_COMPUTE_FUNCTION_2(method)` for one or two scalar inputs.
        `ExtractComputation` must stay compatible with every device the binding may run on.
     4. Document the computation in \ref pyponca_manual_computations, and add it to the test generator
        (see \ref pyponca_tests).

    \subsection pyponca_tests Tests

    The binding is tested by two Python test suites, located in `PyPonca/tests/`:
     - `test_api.py` checks the API rather than the values it returns: mangling and dispatch of point clouds, KD-trees
       and compute objects, construction errors, and calls to `compute` for every compute object, point cloud type and
       filter,
     - `test_accuracy.py` checks that the binding returns the same results as the C++ API. A C++ program
       (`tests/cpp/test3d.cpp`, target `generate_pytests3d`) samples a noisy sphere and random analysis locations, runs
       every method of the Ponca::Factory providing a tested capability through the C++ API, and writes the inputs,
       the status of each fit and its results to `test_cases.json`. The Python test reads this file (its path is given
       by the `TEST_CASES` environment variable), replays every case with %PyPonca on a point cloud, on a strided view
       of it, and on dense and sparse KD-trees, then checks that the fit statuses are identical and that the results
       of `STABLE` fits match (absolute tolerance \f$10^{-8}\f$).

    CNC methods are skipped by `test_accuracy.py`, as their triangle sampling is random. Also note that the test data
    are generated with `std::rand()`, whose sequence differs between platforms: each platform tests a different point
    cloud.

    The tests are registered in CTest with the `pyponca` label when Ponca is configured with
    `PONCA_CONFIGURE_TESTS=ON`. They import the installed `pyponca` module, so the binding must be installed in the
    Python environment found by CMake. From the root of the repository:

    \code{.sh}
        cd PyPonca
        CMAKE_ARGS="-DPONCA_CONFIGURE_TESTS=ON" pip install . --config-settings=build-dir=build
        cd build
        ctest -L pyponca --output-on-failure
    \endcode

   */
}
